ace, the only royal palace in the U.S. It was the official home of the Hawaiian monarchy from 1882 to 1893. The place is all restored and gives a real peek into how the Hawaiian royalty lived — the architecture and gardens were seriously impressive.\n\nThe next day, I headed over to the Polynesian Cultural Center for a killer luau. I got to see some awesome traditional dances and music from different Polynesian cultures, like Hawaiian hula, Samoan fire knife dancing, and Maori poi spinning. The food was next level — kalua pig, lomi salmon, and haupia, a coconut milk dessert, were all hits.\n\nOnce I had my cultural fix on Oahu, I went on to the Big Island to see the natural side of Hawaii. I landed and got my first look at Kilauea, one of the world’s most active volcanoes. I joined a guided hike through Hawaii Volcanoes National Park and watched lava pour into the ocean — it was wild to see the power of nature in action.\n\nAs I kept exploring the Big Island, I was blown away by how varied the landscapes are. From the green rainforests and waterfalls on the Hamakua Coast to the cool black sand beaches at Punalu'u and the snowy top of Mauna Kea, there’s no shortage of stunning views.\n\nOf course, a trip to Hawaii isn’t complete without a visit to Maui. I spent days chilling on the beautiful beaches of Ka’anapali and Wailea, snorkeled with sea turtles in Molokini, and took the famous Road to Hana — full of waterfalls and epic ocean views.\n\nAs I was leaving Hawaii, I felt super grateful for all the awesome stuff I got to experience. From the deep culture of the Hawaiian people to the stunning natural beauty of the islands, Hawaii is a paradise that’s totally worth discovering. So, pack your bags and get ready to say Aloha — Hawaii is calling.", "original_formality": "casual", "bertscore_cf": 0.9093276262283325, "bertscore_dr": 0.844356119632721, "nli_cf": 0.9921060800552368, "nli_dr": 0.961322546005249, "nli_mode_cf": "both", "passed_cf": false, "passed_dr": false}
